## Summary

Browser sign-ins always opened the system default browser. One account needs to sign in with
Edge while the others in the same process keep the default, and on Windows MSAL's
`acquire_token_interactive` can't be told which browser to use. The `BROWSER` environment
variable works, but for the whole process.

This adds `AuthContext(browser=...)`. It takes a program (a name on `PATH` or, as the docs
recommend, an absolute path) or a list of the program and its arguments, such as
`[edge_path, "--profile-directory=Profile 8"]`. When it's set, the context runs MSAL's
sign-in steps itself: `initiate_auth_code_flow`, then MSAL's `AuthCodeReceiver` opening the
address in the registered browser, then `acquire_token_by_auth_code_flow`. The token cache is
written exactly as before. Without `browser`, nothing changes.

`AuthCodeReceiver` lives in `msal.oauth2cli.authcode` and isn't exported from `msal`. It's
the only way to choose a browser per sign-in on Windows, so the reasoning is recorded in
`.local/DECISIONS.md`, and an integration test runs it against the installed msal so that an
upgrade that changes it fails loudly.

The browser is registered with Python's `webbrowser` once per process, under a name made from
a hash of the command, and not as preferred, so other contexts keep the default. A broker
that falls back to the browser uses the chosen one, siblings from `for_tenant` inherit it, and
app flows reject it.

The PR also updates the commit command in `AGENTS.COMMITTING.md` to check the signing key
with `ssh-add -T` before committing, so a commit without the key fails at once instead of
after the pre-commit hooks.

